**What changed**
- **One entry instead of three.** Your credentials are stored as a single encrypted entry, so a load is one read. I estimate about 3.1 s at launch, down from about 6.2 s.
- **Automatic migration.** The first launch reads your old entries, writes the new one and reads it back to check it. It leaves the old entries in place, so you can roll back. That first launch should take roughly 10 to 11 s, once.
- **Cleanup on request.** `EncryptedVault.purge_legacy_entries()` deletes the old entries. It refuses to run until the new entry exists, and it's up to you when to call it.
- **Key-file hardening.** The master key file is now created as 0600 from the first byte, and the folder is 0700. The file is created exclusively, so two threads can't generate different keys, and an empty key file now raises an error instead of silently using a blank key. Your existing key file and encrypted file stay compatible.
- **Saves are verified.** A save is read back, and an error is raised if it didn't stick.
- **The dialog no longer freezes.** Saving keys runs in the background with an "Encrypting and saving…" message. A double tap saves once, and a failed save keeps the dialog open with the error.

